WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.497
Warning! Warning!

2
00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:06.705
I built this with AI and text to speech, because I can't narrate for shit!

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.368
Hey, Dreamer here.

4
00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:15.212
Today, the advanced folder: levelling up, relationships, death, endings and combat.

5
00:00:15.212 --> 00:00:17.818
Lots of little settings, so let's go.

6
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:23.116
Open Mechanics, then Advanced, then Level Progression.

7
00:00:23.116 --> 00:00:26.279
Reaching level two takes a thousand XP here,

8
00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:30.449
and every level after that costs more than the one before.

9
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.402
Characters get an ability point every level, and an attribute point every fifth level.

10
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:41.787
Every tenth level they get a trait pick from the level-up pool.

11
00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:44.275
Ours is empty, so nothing's offered yet.

12
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:46.113
And milestone titles.

13
00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:48.611
Our newcomers start as a First of May,

14
00:00:48.611 --> 00:00:53.737
which is circus talk for a first-season act, and work up to Talk of the Roads.

15
00:00:53.737 --> 00:00:58.665
A title is only given at its exact level, so a skipped level never gets it.

16
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:01.230
Relationship Stages.

17
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:06.870
Every character has a score from minus a hundred to a hundred for how they feel about the player,

18
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.127
and these name the bands, for the player and for the AI.

19
00:01:10.127 --> 00:01:13.441
If you change them, they still have to cover every score.

20
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:21.556
Death Rules. Turn permadeath on and death is permanent: the game ends once the whole party is dead.

21
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.707
Leave it off and a character can come back, played the way these resurrection instructions say.

22
00:01:27.707 --> 00:01:30.891
Studio starts you with a knockout instead of a death,

23
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:35.096
so ours wakes up in the show's camp, stinking of Old Marla's liniment.

24
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.643
End of Game Rules are the director's notes for how a run ends when you win.

25
00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:45.277
Ours ends with a full tent and the crowd on its feet.

26
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:47.781
Then losing, and a neutral end.

27
00:01:47.781 --> 00:01:54.255
None of these happen on their own: a trigger has to end the game, and triggers are coming up next.

28
00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:56.701
Combat Settings.

29
00:01:56.701 --> 00:02:02.453
The first few are about moves characters learn during play: how often they can learn one,

30
00:02:02.453 --> 00:02:07.300
and the tiers the AI sorts them into, each with its own power and cooldown.

31
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:14.577
Combat XP is one reward per NPC tier: fifty for a weak NPC, a hundred for an average one,

32
00:02:14.577 --> 00:02:19.421
and up to a thousand for a mythic, before the level difference.

33
00:02:19.421 --> 00:02:24.573
This part was re-filmed for Voyage's nineteenth of November update,

34
00:02:24.573 --> 00:02:28.110
which replaced the old base combat XP setting.

35
00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:33.730
NPC daily healing is how much health characters in the world get back each day.

36
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.626
The default heals them fully.

37
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:40.705
And damage types are the list every resistance and weakness has to come from.

38
00:02:40.705 --> 00:02:46.487
Spell them exactly the same everywhere: capital F fire and small f fire are two different types,

39
00:02:46.487 --> 00:02:49.198
and a mismatch just quietly does full damage.

40
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.501
That's the advanced mechanics.

41
00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:55.225
Our settings are linked in the description.

42
00:02:55.225 --> 00:02:59.342
Next time, triggers: making things actually happen in your world.

43
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.748
I'm Dreamer. Now go build something weird, and I'll see you next time!

